==English==
{{was wotd|2010|March|18}}

===Etymology===
{{bor+|en|fr|friable}}, from {{der|en|la|friābilis|t=friable}}, from {{m|la|friō|t=I crumble}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|frī'ə-bəl}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈfɹaɪəbl̩/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-friable.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪəbəl|s=3}}
* {{homophones|en|fryable}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Easily broken into small [[fragment]]s, [[crumble]]d, or reduced to [[powder]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1977|author={{w|Angela Carter}}|title=The Passion of New Eve|text=Spiders had woven their vague trapezes between the '''friable''' heads of dead peonies in enormous glass jars streaked with tide marks where the water had evaporated long ago.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1983|author={{w|Lawrence Durrell}}|title=Sebastian|publisher=Faber & Faber|year_published=2004|edition=Avignon Quintet|page=1020|text=This light, '''friable''' type of material offered excellent insulation against both desert heat and also the cold of darkness during the winter.}}
# {{lb|en|of soil}} [[loose|Loose]] and large-grained in [[consistency]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1890|author=James George Frazer|title=The Golden Bough|text=So while two men under his directions were digging the grave with sticks in the '''friable''' granitic soil, he superintended the costume of the other actors in the drama.}}
# {{lb|en|of [[poison|poisons]]}} Likely to crumble and become [[airborne]], thus becoming a [[health]] [[risk]]
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=April 1987|journal=Old-House Journal|text=It is when [[asbestos]]-containing products are '''friable''' that hazardous asbestos fibers are likely to be released and sent airborne.}}
# {{lb|en|math|of a number}} [[smooth|Smooth]]: that [[factor]]s completely into small [[prime number]]s.

